Z||000003||lou_dias||08-28-2001||10:35 AM||||mY 88gt needs a new clutch also... any suggestions focussing on low-balling the cost?||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||08-28-2001||12:08 PM||||A LUK pressure plate is the same no matter how much you pay for it or what color it is painted. <BR>The three kits I've installed all had the EXACT same LUK pressure plate, only one came from a GM dealer (close to $400, boy was I naive then), one from RAM (via Summitt, $140), and one from a local machine shop ($120, same as from Pep Boys, only they turned the flywheel for free).  All were brand new parts, no rebuilds.  So far, each has been good for about 60,000 miles of abuse from me.<BR>Most shops will price a Fiero clutch job at about 8 or so hours of labor, at whatever their hourly rate is.  I paid the dealer in case #1 almost $800 back in 1990 for my first clutch.  I've done them myself since then, since there i usually something else like a rear main seal to replace, or transaxle mounts, poly bushings, exhaust manifold gaskets, etc, that is easiest done with the engine out.<BR>There are a few specialty clutches in use/for sale on this board, but I don't think they would be worth it for a stock daily driver V-6. These things aren't that powerful.  <BR>Some brands (RAM) will even give you a lifetime warranty, but you still have to pay for the labor again.<BR>  <BR>Just my opinion.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||My7Fieros||08-28-2001||04:10 PM||||I may have gotten bad info, but I heard that Fieros are notorious for "rejecting" aftermarket clutches. Like Indy resto said, Borg Warner is the one to go w/ from what Ive heard. However Ive also heard that a OEM GM Fiero clutch is discontinued....hmmm. Clutches arent my thing, so dont hold me to this..........<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
